WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.920
You are the only one who can save the planet, battle with aliens at the speed exceeding the speed of light, in dark unknown space, Defender 2.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.760
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.420
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.640
And I'm Joe. Wow, hello everybody.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.480
How you guys doing?

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.540
I'm doing great. When you were playing this game, were you under the assumption that you were exceeding the speed of light?

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:49.080
Entirely so. Because you see, when you are going through the speed of light, everything gets blue shifted, and there's a lot of blue.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.780
There is a lot of blue, but there's still a lot of red also in the game.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.140
That's the things behind you.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.780
The thing behind you gets red shifted.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.880
There's more black than anything.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.560
What are you guys talking about? Black holes go faster than the speed of light.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.480
No, it's really, really dark blue.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.940
Got it. True. Yeah, that's true.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.860
I always lean, is it dark blue or is it black?

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.280
Yeah. I understand. It's dark yellow.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.580
I'm just glad that we're playing some hard sci-fi, finally.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:16.600
Yeah, yeah. So we are playing Defender 2, originally released way back in 1981 as Stargate.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.100
Bet you guys didn't know that.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:24.260
I did not. So it only got the Defender 2 name for the NES and then everything afterwards, because I

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.900
guess there was some legal problems with saying Stargate.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.760
I'm not too familiar with Star Trek, but would that have been the problem?

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:37.040
There is a movie and TV series called Stargate, but I don't think it was active in 1981.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.780
Got it. And I actually did read a funny anecdote about it being called Stargate.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.740
Please. That's weird that it's just someone else's opinion that I agree with, that it's weird that

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:51.720
it's called Defender 2 on the NES that doesn't have a Defender 1 and then changed to Stargate

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.980
in arcades where it actually has a Defender 1 and not called Defender 2.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.700
Right. And...

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.720
Yikes. Because in the arcade, this actually, this comes out seven months after Defender 1.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.840
So that is pretty crazy that like they release it in the arcade and then it takes them just

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.560
seven months to make this new game that they call Stargate.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:14.400
But if you've played Defender 1, you'll see it right away that this is very much a Defender 2.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.160
It plays exactly like Defender.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.560
Would you say it's...

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.860
I mean, I didn't play it, but I watched Defender 1.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.140
And would you say it's like...

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.160
plays like Defender 1 or would you just say it's Defender 1?

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.640
I would say it's Defender 1 with two new power-ups.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.940
Yeah, because it looks like almost exactly...

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.140
I couldn't even find the difference.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:34.460
Yeah, it's also Defender 1 without any kind of processing power behind it.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:40.220
Yeah. Because somehow a 1981 game performs a little bit better than this 1988 game.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:46.000
Yeah, that's true. I don't know how they couldn't figure out how to just perfectly convert the arcade version to

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.340
the NES here. We have seen some better arcade ports from like later arcade games.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.260
I would even argue like a game like Double Dragon.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.520
Yeah. Translated it really well from the arcade.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.700
But I think we're going to get ahead of ourselves here.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:04.820
Let's just focus on Defender 1 for a minute since a large amount of our audience probably knows of that game too.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.620
That was a considered very difficult arcade game at the time.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:14.000
A little different than what you were playing if you were playing Pong or Pac-Man or Missile Command.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.900
This one had a lot of moving parts to it.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:21.880
Did either one of you guys get a chance to play Defender 1 ever in an arcade or see it?

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.800
I haven't actually played it, but I was watching some footage of it.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.720
It's, I mean, in 1981, I would have had a blast with that.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.580
Yeah, it was actually like my mom's favorite Atari 2600 game.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.300
We owned an Atari 2600 and she would always talk about the game.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.540
Always. And I was too, well, I just think it's funny.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.140
It's like whenever we had the Atari 2600 out, she just wanted to play Defender.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.380
And I never heard of that in my like cultural zeitgeist.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.860
So I was like, all right, I'll try this out.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.060
And I was way too young to be any good at it.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:56.440
Like it didn't make, it didn't really make any sense at my young age of like four or five or however young I was.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.840
But Defender doesn't really have the clout that other arcade games do.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:02.760
Do you agree? Why?

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.820
Like you don't hear that name anymore.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.000
I mean, you guys talking about it, like, oh, everyone knows Defender.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:08.940
I don't think I knew Defender.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:09.880
I didn't say that.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.320
Well, I just assumed you and Mike are the same person.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.600
I think like that's a game that I picture in an arcade lineup.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:21.580
You know, if I walk into an arcade, I'm kind of expecting it to be there, but it doesn't really have a place now.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.220
Like no one talks about it as much.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:30.440
And I think that's maybe because this was made by Williams, who really only brought us Joust and Robotron.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:35.080
And I'd argue you're more likely to even see Joust than you would Defender in an arcade these days.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.400
I know Joust. Most people, I think, would know Joust over Defender.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.980
And we've talked about Joust before in relation to Balloon Fight.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.420
So sure. I think Iwata was like inspired by it when.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.280
Ah, yes. All right.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:50.900
We'll get into it, though.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.600
I don't want to stall any longer.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.320
We'll talk about Defender 2 if we must.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:03.180
First, the player flies a small spaceship above a scrolling mountainous landscape, which wraps around conveniently.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.660
You try to save humans from being captured by enemy ships.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:10.820
And the goal is to destroy all the enemies and prevent the humans from being captured.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.300
You're armed with ammo and smart bombs.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.260
Oh, and there's this little thing called Stargates, but we'll get to those in a minute.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:17.940
Did I do a good job?

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:19.740
I think so. You did a great job, Mike.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:20.960
Thanks. I wrote it down beforehand.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.120
Oh, scripted. Well, we have trouble with the overviews sometimes.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:25.520
I figured, let me just write this.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:33.080
I can't deny that. One thing that I really note that jumped out to me right away was because we always talk about shmups and how we, at least I don't like them.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:39.440
But this stood out from the rest in the sense that, first of all, you're not constantly scrolling in one direction.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:47.780
You can turn around. I know it doesn't sound like that mind-blowing of like a crazy ability, but it's kind of cool to me that you can choose to go left or right and backtrack on yourself.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.660
And that you have a different goal that's not just kill everything or like get racked up as many points.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.880
Like you're trying to save these humans on the ground.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.660
So I thought that made it like, at least at first, stand out to me more than like a lot of other shmups.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:09.040
Would you argue maybes, and we haven't even gotten to the controls yet, but do you think there's too many things going on here though?

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:14.980
Like you have a screen that you have to kind of follow along in a mini-map to see your placement in regards to the enemies.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.580
Then you have the enemies, which you must take care of to clear the stage.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:24.360
And then you have these humans, which you can't see on your mini-map, but you have to make sure that they're being protected at all times.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:27.480
I think definitely that the mini-map, I think it's cool.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:33.600
But yeah, it's definitely missing out on something with like not having your objectives on there, the humans on there.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.180
But I don't think it took away anything.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:40.800
I mean, not having the mini-map, I think would have been, I prefer having it to not having it, even if it's just showing the enemies.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:43.860
It was a few times when I was just trying to get that like last ship.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.100
Yeah. You know, it was cool to be able to see like, oh, I'm coming up on where he is.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:58.520
The game can be pretty chaotic, but I don't think that any one element of like the HUD in the game itself really overwhelms you too much.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.060
Yeah. I actually think I would have preferred more things on the HUD, like a human count.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:07.180
Yeah. As we're going through, because I think you get five?

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:08.680
I think there's six.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.920
Six, okay. And there's no real way.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.640
Six people on the ground. Yeah, six people on the ground.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:16.220
And there's no real way of knowing until they're picked up by an alien.

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:20.340
Then they, I think their shape changes color on the HUD.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:24.120
Oh. The alien enemy shape changes to like a different tint.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.830
But there was no real sense of like, where am I going?

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:38.000
Yeah. Am I heading? You know, you just kind of have to constantly like travel fast to the other sides to make sure that the other humans haven't been kidnapped.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:46.800
To clarify, in this game, when humans are kidnapped, it's not like, you know, once they are, once an alien grabs them, they're gone forever.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:51.340
It's actually sort of like a grabber machine.

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:56.260
Like, what are those things when you put a quarter in and you're trying to-

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:57.340
Yeah, like the claw. The claw. The crane game.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:04.820
Yeah, crane. Where they just pick up a guy and try to take him outside of your field of view.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.980
So you can shoot whichever alien is holding him.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.900
And then they'll just sort of very slowly float back to the ground and you can pick them up.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.820
But you can also accidentally shoot the person.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:18.900
Shoot the person. I appreciated that feature.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:23.320
Yeah. You know, because it is, it's a lot easier to do than you would think.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:28.380
Yeah. You, you have a bit of a stuttery control of your, of your spaceship.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:30.560
You don't have, you don't have a ton of precision.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:31.440
You're floaty.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:37.580
Yeah. So it's very easy to approach an enemy, shoot it, and then it continues climbing up.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:41.440
So you've actually, your laser has now shot the, the humanoid.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:45.300
Yeah. And they burst into a million pieces and it's kind of, it's kind of good.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:49.500
Like I, I like the feeling of it, even though it's, it's bad for my point total.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.140
Sick. All right.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.200
The one thing that I did notice was that it took me a while to notice actually at first

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.380
when I was playing was that when the, when they slowly dropped to the ground, as you mentioned,

00:08:58.640 --> 00:08:59.680
that's not a good thing.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:00.780
I thought that that was okay.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.240
They slowly dropped to the ground and they're okay.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.280
They're not. I think they splatter, right?

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:06.200
They die. I mean, I assume they die.

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.040
They disappear. They do.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.180
They go away. It doesn't count towards your bonus.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:11.580
They go away. Yeah.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:14.060
They go away. Put it nicely for the children.

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:15.380
They go away.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:19.480
Um, but yeah, you have to catch them in your ship, which took me like a single day.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.340
I can play through on like the first round and realize, oh, this is what I'm.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:27.640
And I never caught an alien in the act of just saving a human, but in the arcade version

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:33.540
at low heights, the humanoids do like fall and survive, but at a certain height, it will

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:38.240
kill them. I never got to see like an alien that didn't have them at like mid height already.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:43.120
Yeah. So I can't confirm if that stayed in the NES version, but a couple of things didn't make

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:44.300
it over from the arcade version.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:47.540
So I wouldn't be surprised if that was also something that was just sacrificed in favor

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:49.740
of just coding. They always die if you don't catch them.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:51.420
Yeah. It's not something that I noticed either.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.080
So yeah, it would be kind of unfair if like, if they're like a foot off the ground and you

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:58.880
shoot the ship, you're not gonna have time to catch them before they hit the ground.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.880
Right. So hopefully they would be saved.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.480
Uh, and then the stargate, which, uh, if we can talk about that for a minute, that is

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:11.120
that black hole like thing that will transport you to the other square, black square.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.720
That is not black, but yeah, it's actually many rainbow colors.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:19.180
Uh, it'll transport you to the other side of the screen that you're on.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:24.700
And my understanding is, is that in the arcade version that actually warped you to a human

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:26.120
in trouble. Okay.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:28.000
But that definitely is not the case here.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:32.100
If you find the stargate and you go over to the other side, there might be nothing over

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:37.360
there. Honestly, I didn't, I didn't use this at all because there's no point of reference in this

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:43.820
map. Like there are, there are squiggly lines to, to show, uh, relief and where mountains may

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.320
be, but none of it feels like a place.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:52.700
It's, it's just like, once I go into this portal, I just get spit out somewhere sort of,

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.500
sort of different, but exactly the same.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:58.240
Yeah. Um, so I never used it on purpose.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:00.040
I just accidentally fell into it sometimes.

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:06.720
Yeah. It didn't seem like there was any real use to it except for a buffering mechanic to like,

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.560
be like, Oh, well everything will freeze in time.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:12.660
While I get this annoying animation that transports me to the other end of the screen.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:18.000
It's worth mentioning that we're not talking about levels here because the waves are all

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:21.480
the same. Just more enemies come on wave after wave.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.760
You, the landscapes don't change at all.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:27.080
Nothing, nothing about the game visually looks different.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:34.940
And I think that it's weird that we're getting such a bare bones arcade experience from 1981

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.640
here now sold at like, as like a full fledged game.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:39.940
I agree.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:41.400
I don't know.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:45.080
Uh, so a little story of what I expected from this game.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:52.180
Mm hmm. I saw, I saw screenshots first and screenshots actually got me a little excited because it

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:56.100
almost looked like a, like a vector graphics thing with, it's almost sort of looked like

00:11:56.100 --> 00:12:02.880
asteroids. And I also saw your little ship and I'm like, Oh, maybe this game sort of, it sort of feels

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:09.440
like asteroids. Um, so I booted it up and there's, yeah, there's some momentum based controls, but at the end

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:13.700
of the day, it is just a very, um, small scope shmup.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:20.520
But I would have really liked the game where it's like, you know, that whole, uh, inertia

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:22.400
based control completely.

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:24.840
And you just sort of have the tank controls.

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:26.960
That's what I wanted this game to be.

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:28.820
And it really wasn't that.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:35.340
But, and, and tapping on, on the inertia controls there, I wasn't, I wasn't bothered by them

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:38.760
at first, but the more I played the game, the more I realized they are my demise.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:43.160
And this game for a game that talks about going faster than light speed, unless you're

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:47.540
very good at this game, it does not reward you for, for going fast.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:51.460
